File: octave.info,  Node: Keywords,  Next: Parser,  Up: Grammar and Parser

F.1 Keywords
============

The identifiers below are keywords, and may not be used as variable or
function names:

Categories:
...........

*note Utility Functions: XREFkwcategoryUtilityFunctions. | *note
Variable Declaration: XREFkwcategoryVariableDeclaration. | *note
Function Definition: XREFkwcategoryFunctionDefinition. | *note
Control Statements: XREFkwcategoryControlStructures. | *note
Iterating Structures: XREFkwcategoryIteratingStructures. | *note
Classdef Structures: XREFkwcategoryClassdefStructures. | *note
Execution Environment: XREFkwcategoryExecutionEnvironment.

Alphabetical keyword listing:
.............................

*note __FILE__: XREF__FILE__. | *note __LINE__: XREF__LINE__. | *note
break: XREFbreak. | *note case: XREFcase. | *note catch: XREFcatch. |
*note classdef: XREFclassdef. | *note continue: XREFcontinue. | *note
do: XREFdo. | *note else: XREFelse. | *note elseif: XREFelseif. | *note
end: XREFend. | *note end_try_catch: XREFend_try_catch. | *note
end_unwind_protect: XREFend_unwind_protect. | *note endclassdef:
XREFendclassdef. | *note endenumeration: XREFendenumeration. | *note
endevents: XREFendevents. | *note endfor: XREFendfor. | *note
endfunction: XREFendfunction. | *note endif: XREFendif. | *note
endmethods: XREFendmethods. | *note endparfor: XREFendparfor. | *note
endproperties: XREFendproperties. | *note endswitch: XREFendswitch. |
*note endwhile: XREFendwhile. | *note endenumeration: XREFenumeration. |
*note events: XREFevents. | *note for: XREFfor. | *note function:
XREFfunction. | *note global: XREFglobal. | *note if: XREFif. | *note
methods: XREFkwpropertymethods. | *note otherwise: XREFotherwise. |
*note parfor: XREFparfor. | *note persistent: XREFpersistent. | *note
properties: XREFkwpropertyproperties. | *note return: XREFreturn. |
*note switch: XREFswitch. | *note try: XREFtry. | *note until:
XREFuntil. | *note unwind_protect: XREFunwind_protect. | *note
unwind_protect_cleanup: XREFunwind_protect_cleanup. | *note while:
XREFwhile.

Utility Functions:
------------------

The function ‘iskeyword’ can be used to quickly check whether an
identifier is reserved by Octave.

 -- : iskeyword ()
 -- : iskeyword (NAME)
     Return true if NAME is an Octave keyword.

     If NAME is omitted, return a list of keywords.

     See also: *note isvarname: XREFisvarname, *note exist: XREFexist.

Variable Declaration:
---------------------

 -- : global VAR
     Declare variables to have global scope.

          global X;
          if (isempty (X))
            x = 1;
          endif

     See also: *note persistent: XREFpersistent.

 -- : persistent VAR
     Declare variables as persistent.

     A variable that has been declared persistent within a function will
     retain its contents in memory between subsequent calls to the same
     function.  The difference between persistent variables and global
     variables is that persistent variables are local in scope to a
     particular function and are not visible elsewhere.

     See also: *note global: XREFglobal.

Function Definition:
--------------------

 -- : function OUTPUTS = function_name (INPUT, ...)
 -- : function function_name (INPUT, ...)
 -- : function OUTPUTS = function_name
     Begin a function body with name ‘function_name’, with OUTPUTS as
     results, and with INPUTS as parameters.

     The function can later be invoked in Octave using the syntax

          [OUTPUT1, OUTPUT2, ...] = function_name (INPUT1, INPUT2, ...)

     See also: *note return: XREFreturn.

 -- : endfunction
     Mark the end of a function.

     See also: *note function: XREFfunction.

 -- : end
     Last element of an array or the end of any ‘for’, ‘parfor’, ‘if’,
     ‘do’, ‘while’, ‘function’, ‘switch’, ‘try’, or ‘unwind_protect’
     block.

     As an index of an array, the magic index "end" refers to the last
     valid entry in an indexing operation.

     Example:

          X = [ 1 2 3; 4 5 6 ];
          X(1,end)
           ⇒ 3
          X(end,1)
           ⇒ 4
          X(end,end)
           ⇒ 6

     Programming notes:
       1. The ‘end’ keyword cannot be used within ‘subsref’, ‘subsasgn’,
          or ‘substruct’ for manual indexing operations.

       2. For custom classes, to enable use of ‘end’ in indexing
          expressions it must be overloaded with a function definition
          such as:
               function last_index = end (obj, end_dim, ndim_obj)
                 if (end_dim == ndim_obj)
                   last_index = prod (size (obj)(end_dim:ndim_obj));
                 else
                   last_index = size (obj, end_dim);
                 endif
               endfunction
          For more information see *note Object Oriented Programming:
          Object Oriented Programming.

     See also: *note for: XREFfor, *note parfor: XREFparfor, *note if:
     XREFif, *note do: XREFdo, *note while: XREFwhile, *note function:
     XREFfunction, *note switch: XREFswitch, *note try: XREFtry, *note
     unwind_protect: XREFunwind_protect.

 -- : return
     Return execution control immediately from a function or script to
     the calling code.

     ‘return’ is used to stop executing code and exit an m-file
     immediately rather than continuing until the end of the function or
     script is reached.

     If the function or script was invoked directly, rather than from
     calling code in an m-file, then Octave returns to the command line.

     See also: *note function: XREFfunction.

Control Statements:
-------------------

 -- : if (COND) ... endif
 -- : if (COND) ... else ... endif
 -- : if (COND) ... elseif (COND) ... endif
 -- : if (COND) ... elseif (COND) ... else ... endif
     Begin an if block.

     The conditional COND is true if it is not empty and if _all_ values
     are nonzero.

          x = 1;
          if (x == 1)
            disp ("one");
          elseif (x == 2)
            disp ("two");
          else
            disp ("not one or two");
          endif

     See also: *note switch: XREFswitch.

 -- : else
     Alternate action for an if block.

     See ‘if’ for an example.

     See also: *note if: XREFif.

 -- : elseif (COND)
     Alternate conditional test for an if block.

     The conditional COND is true if it is not empty and if _all_ values
     are nonzero.

     See ‘if’ for an example.

     See also: *note if: XREFif.

 -- : endif
     Mark the end of an if block.

     See ‘if’ for an example.

     See also: *note if: XREFif.

 -- : switch STATEMENT
     Begin a switch block.

          yesno = "yes";

          switch (yesno)
            case {"Yes" "yes" "YES" "y" "Y"}
              value = 1;
            case {"No" "no" "NO" "n" "N"}
              value = 0;
            otherwise
              error ("invalid value");
          endswitch

     See also: *note if: XREFif, *note case: XREFcase, *note otherwise:
     XREFotherwise.

 -- : case VALUE
 -- : case {VALUE, ...}
     A case statement in a switch block.

     Octave cases are exclusive and do not fall-through as do C-language
     cases.  A switch statement must have at least one case.  See
     ‘switch’ for an example.

     See also: *note switch: XREFswitch.

 -- : otherwise
     The default statement in a switch block which is executed when no
     other case statements match the input.

     See also: *note switch: XREFswitch, *note case: XREFcase.

 -- : endswitch
     Mark the end of a switch block.

     See ‘switch’ for an example.

     See also: *note switch: XREFswitch.

 -- : try
     Begin a try-catch block.

     If an error occurs within a try block, then the catch code will be
     run and execution will proceed after the catch block (though it is
     often recommended to use the ‘lasterr’ function to re-throw the
     error after cleanup is completed).

     See also: *note catch: XREFcatch, *note unwind_protect:
     XREFunwind_protect.

 -- : catch
 -- : catch VALUE
     Begin the cleanup part of a try-catch block.

     See also: *note try: XREFtry.

 -- : end_try_catch
     Mark the end of a ‘try-catch’ block.

     See also: *note try: XREFtry, *note catch: XREFcatch.

 -- : unwind_protect
     Begin an unwind_protect block.

     If an error occurs within the first part of an unwind_protect block
     the commands within the unwind_protect_cleanup block are executed
     before the error is thrown.  If an error is not thrown, then the
     unwind_protect_cleanup block is still executed.  In other words,
     the unwind_protect_cleanup code is guaranteed to execute regardless
     of success or failure in the unwind_protect block.

     See also: *note unwind_protect_cleanup: XREFunwind_protect_cleanup,
     *note try: XREFtry.

 -- : unwind_protect_cleanup
     Begin the cleanup section of an unwind_protect block.

     See also: *note unwind_protect: XREFunwind_protect.

 -- : end_unwind_protect
     Mark the end of an unwind_protect block.

     See also: *note unwind_protect: XREFunwind_protect.

Iterating Structures:
---------------------

 -- : for I = RANGE
     Begin a for loop.

          for i = 1:10
            i
          endfor

     See also: *note parfor: XREFparfor, *note do: XREFdo, *note while:
     XREFwhile.

 -- : endfor
     Mark the end of a for loop.

     See ‘for’ for an example.

     See also: *note for: XREFfor.

 -- : while (COND)
     Begin a while loop.

     The conditional COND is true if it is not empty and if _all_ values
     are nonzero.

          i = 0;
          while (i < 10)
            i++
          endwhile

     See also: *note do: XREFdo, *note endwhile: XREFendwhile, *note
     for: XREFfor, *note until: XREFuntil.

 -- : endwhile
     Mark the end of a while loop.

     See ‘while’ for an example.

     See also: *note do: XREFdo, *note while: XREFwhile.

 -- : do
     Begin a do-until loop.

     This differs from a while loop in that the body of the loop is
     executed at least once.

          i = 0;
          do
            i++
          until (i == 10)

     See also: *note for: XREFfor, *note until: XREFuntil, *note while:
     XREFwhile.

 -- : until (COND)
     End a do-until loop.

     The conditional COND is true if it is not empty and if _all_ values
     are nonzero.

     See ‘do’ for an example.

     See also: *note do: XREFdo.

 -- : parfor I = RANGE
 -- : parfor (I = RANGE, MAXPROC)
     Begin a for loop that may execute in parallel.

     A ‘parfor’ loop has the same syntax as a ‘for’ loop.  If your
     Octave session has a parallel processing pool enabled, the
     iterations of the ‘parfor’ loop will be executed in parallel across
     the pool's workers.  Otherwise, ‘parfor’ will behave exactly as
     ‘for’.

     When operating in parallel mode, a ‘parfor’ loop's iterations are
     not guaranteed to occur sequentially, and there are additional
     restrictions about the data access operations you can do inside the
     loop body.

     *Warning:* parallel processing pools are currently unimplemented in
     Octave; ‘parfor’ currently behaves exactly as a normal ‘for’ loop.

          parfor i = 1:10
            i
          endparfor

     See also: *note for: XREFfor, *note do: XREFdo, *note while:
     XREFwhile.

 -- : endparfor
     Mark the end of a parfor loop.

     See ‘parfor’ for an example.

     See also: *note parfor: XREFparfor.

 -- : break
     Exit the innermost enclosing do, while, or for loop.

     See also: *note do: XREFdo, *note while: XREFwhile, *note for:
     XREFfor, *note parfor: XREFparfor, *note continue: XREFcontinue.

 -- : continue
     Jump to the end of the innermost enclosing do, while, or for loop.

     See also: *note break: XREFbreak, *note do: XREFdo, *note while:
     XREFwhile, *note for: XREFfor, *note parfor: XREFparfor.

Classdef Structures:
--------------------

 -- : classdef
     Begin a classdef block.

     See also: *note properties: XREFproperties, *note methods:
     XREFmethods, *note events: XREFevents, *note enumeration:
     XREFenumeration.

 -- : endclassdef
     Mark the end of a classdef definition.

     See also: *note classdef: XREFclassdef.

 -- : properties
     Mark the beginning of a block of properties in a classdef
     definition.  Note that the *note function "properties":
     XREFproperties. is a function that lists the properties of a
     classdef class or object.

     See also: *note endproperties: XREFendproperties.

 -- : endproperties
     Mark the end of a properties block in a classdef definition.

     See also: *note properties: XREFproperties.

 -- : methods
     Mark the beginning of a block of methods in a classdef definition.
     Note that the *note function "methods": XREFmethods. is a function
     that lists the methods of a class or object.

     See also: *note endmethods: XREFendmethods.

 -- : endmethods
     Mark the end of a methods block in a classdef definition.

     See also: *note methods: XREFmethods.

 -- : events
     Begin an events block in a classdef definition.

 -- : endevents
     Mark the end of an events block in a classdef definition.

     See also: *note events: XREFevents.

 -- : enumeration
     Begin an enumeration block in a classdef definition.

 -- : endenumeration
     Mark the end of an enumeration block in a classdef definition.

     See also: *note enumeration: XREFenumeration.

Execution Environment:
----------------------

 -- : __FILE__
     When the lexer recognizes the "__FILE__" keyword it returns a
     character array containing the full name and path of the file that
     is being executed.  "__FILE__" will return ‘stdin’ if called from
     the command line.

     See also: *note __LINE__: XREF__LINE__.

 -- : __LINE__
     When the lexer recognizes the "__LINE__" keyword it returns a
     numeric value containing the current input line number of the
     function or file being executed.  "__LINE__" will return ‘1’ if
     called from the command line.

     See also: *note __FILE__: XREF__FILE__.


File: octave.info,  Node: Parser,  Prev: Keywords,  Up: Grammar and Parser

F.2 Parser
==========

The parser has a number of variables that affect its internal operation.
These variables are generally documented in the manual alongside the
code that they affect.

   In addition, there are three non-specific parser customization
functions.  ‘add_input_event_hook’ can be used to schedule a user
function for periodic evaluation.  ‘remove_input_event_hook’ will stop a
user function from being evaluated periodically.

 -- : ID = add_input_event_hook (FCN)
 -- : ID = add_input_event_hook (FCN, DATA)
     Add the named function or function handle FCN to the list of
     functions to call periodically when Octave is waiting for input.

     The function should have the form

          FCN (DATA)

     If DATA is omitted, Octave calls the function without any
     arguments.

     The returned identifier may be used to remove the function handle
     from the list of input hook functions.

     See also: *note remove_input_event_hook:
     XREFremove_input_event_hook.

 -- : remove_input_event_hook (NAME)
 -- : remove_input_event_hook (FCN_ID)
     Remove the named function or function handle with the given
     identifier from the list of functions to call periodically when
     Octave is waiting for input.

     See also: *note add_input_event_hook: XREFadd_input_event_hook.

   Finally, when the parser cannot identify an input token it calls a
particular function to handle this.  By default, this is the internal
function "__unimplemented__" which makes suggestions about possible
Octave substitutes for MATLAB functions.

 -- : VAL = missing_function_hook ()
 -- : OLD_VAL = missing_function_hook (NEW_VAL)
 -- : OLD_VAL = missing_function_hook (NEW_VAL, "local")
     Query or set the internal variable that specifies the function to
     call to provide extra information when an unknown identifier is
     referenced.

     When called from inside a function with the "local" option, the
     variable is changed locally for the function and any subroutines it
     calls.  The original variable value is restored when exiting the
     function.

     See also: *note missing_component_hook: XREFmissing_component_hook.


